  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nA pattern change with cooler and drier air across the Northland mid-\nweek, then a return to warmer temperatures late week into next \nweekend. Occasional chances for precipitation, but no significant \nprecipitation amounts nor signal for severe storms through the next \n5-7 days, with long-range outlooks heavily favored toward above \nnormal temperatures through the end of July.\n\nA secondary cold front moves north to south tonight through the\nregion bringing low level moisture along the front and cooler \nand drier air in its wake through mid-week as high pressure \nbuilds in with north-northwesterly flow aloft. The airmass aloft\nis so cool with 850mb temps of +2-6C it is actually below the \ntenth percentile of climatology for the International Falls RAOB\nclimatology for this time of year.\n\nTonight the final cold front moves north to south bringing with\nit low level moisture/clouds and then drier air in its wake. \nThis will lead to cloud cover overnight into Tuesday with light \nrain showers (or perhaps even more like drizzle/sprinkles \ntowards Tuesday morning) but overall little in the way of \nmeasurable precipitation. \n\nWith the cooler airmass tomorrow highs will range from the mid 60s \nin the MN Arrowhead to the 70s elsewhere, with the Duluth forecast \nof 72 at the airport being the coolest day of July (coolest since 71 \non June 29) - and it'll even be 66 near the lake! Gradually warming \nup through mid week as an area of high pressure builds in. \n\nLate-week there will be a broad upper level ridge over the western \nCONUS and a deep-level trough over eastern Canada and the northeast \nleading to northwesterly flow aloft for the Upper Midwest. In this \nnorthwesterly flow there will be a few weak mid-level impulses with \nincreasing low level temps in the westerly flow at low levels. This \nwill lead to a few chances for precipitation late week into next \nweekend. Timing is tough to nail down due to the range in guidance, \nbut generally most scenarios look to be light/limited precip and no \nstrong signal for organized severe storms. Precipitation amounts at \nbest are likely less than quarter inch over the weekend (70-85% of \nensemble guidance). Highs back into the upper 80s and low 90s \nSaturday and Sunday, and Heat Advisories will likely needed for \nparts of the Northland.",
